  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Brisk and cooler today\n\nFront is east of us and getting some uptick in winds in the\nmountains. Should see this trend continue through the day with\nupslope cloudiness around into the morning and part of the\nafternoon, with drier air scattering the skies out some, but\npotential for more upslope clouds tonight with another\nshortwave.\n\nWinds into the day Saturday may gust 20 to 30 mph over the \nmountains. Over the Piedmont, gusts of 10 to 20 mph will be \ncommon, before subsiding during the late afternoon into the \nevening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry and trending milder through the middle of next \nweek.\n\nOur weather pattern is expected to remain one dominated by generally \nnorthwest flow aloft through the the middle portion of next week. \nWhile a series of low pressure systems are expected to cross the \nGreat Lakes region into New England, the moisture and energy \nassociated with them is expected to remain to the north. Our coolest \ntemperatures are expected on Sunday with values near normal for this \ntime of year. As we progress into mid-week, geopotential heights are \nexpected to gradually rises, and so too the surface temperatures. By \nWednesday and Thursday, temperatures are expected to be five to ten \ndegrees above normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cooler temperatures and a return of precipitation to \nconclude the week.\n\nWhile specific timing of features still is in question, the general \ntrend among most model guidance is for a longwave trough to dig into \nthe Mississippi Valley on Thursday and progress east towards or into \nour region by Friday. The result will be a trend towards lower \ntemperatures and better chance of precipitation for the area.",
